.Dd August 16, 2026
.Dt CTRLMENU 3
.Os libcontrol
.Sh NAME
.Nm ctrlmenu_popup
.Nd pop-up menu routines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In X11/Xlib.h
.In X11/Xresource.h
.In control/application.h
.In control/menu.h
.Bd -literal
struct ctrlmenu {
	/* caller program must set these: */
	char const *title;
	struct ctrlmenu_entry *entries;
	size_t nentries;

	/* caller program can get these: */
	Window window;

	/* caller program shall ignore remaining members */
};

struct ctrlmenu_entry {
	enum {
		CTRLMENU_COMMAND,
		CTRLMENU_SEPARATOR,
		CTRLMENU_CASCADE,
	} type;

	char *label;
	bool disabled;

	void (*callback)(void *ctx, void *self, bool alt);
	void *self;

	struct ctrlmenu *submenu;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Ft void
.Fo ctrlmenu_popup
.Fa "void *context"
.Fa "Display *display"
.Fa "Drawable reference"
.Fa "XrmDatabase resurces"
.Fa "Window leader"
.Fa "int x_root"
.Fa "int y_root"
.Fa "Time timestamp"
.Fa "struct ctrlmenu *menu"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo ctrlmenu_tearoff
.Fa "Display *display"
.Fa "Drawable reference"
.Fa "XrmDatabase resurces"
.Fa "Window leader"
.Fa "int x_root"
.Fa "int y_root"
.Fa "struct ctrlmenu *menu"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The ctrlmenu library implements both a pop-up menu
(used as context menu, or start menu, for example),
and a torn-off menu.
.Pp
The menu windows created by these routines are controlled by the user
(see the section
.Sx USAGE
below),
and are configured with X resources
(see the section
.Sx RESOURCES
below).
For resources to be usable,
the program must call
.Xr ctrlapp_set 3
to set a global name and class for the application.
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlmenu_popup
function creates a pop-up menu on an override-redirect window
(i'e', not managed by the window manager)
for the given
.Fa menu .
It blocks as long as the menu is popped up
(i'e', until an entry is selected, a menu tears off, or the menu is closed).
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlmenu_tearoff
function creates a torn-off menu on a framed/regular window
(i'e', managed by the window manager)
for the given
.Fa menu .
The entry
.Fa menu->window
is filled with the mapped window that contains the menu.
Whenever the caller program gets an
.Xr XEvent 3
for this window, it must call
.Fa ctrlmenu_handle_event
on this
.Fa menu .
.Pp
The
.Fn ctrlmenu_handle_event
function handles an
.Fa event
received for the given
.Fa menu .
A menu tears off
either because the user clicked on the tear-off entry of a pop-up menu created by
.Fn ctrlmenu_popup ,
or the menu was already created as such by
.Fn ctrlmenu_tearoff .
Whenever the caller program gets an event for a window it does not directly manage,
it must loop through all its
.Ft "struct ctrlmenu"
objects, check if the
.Fa ".window"
member matches the event's window, and call
.Fn ctrlmenu_handle_event
on it.
.Ss Parameters
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa context
The pointer to the context of the caller program.
It is passed to the
.Fa callback
routine defined by the caller.
How the context is used for by the callback routine for is up to the program.
.It Fa display
The pointer to the context of the connection to the
.Xr Xserver 1 .
.It Fa menu
The pointer to a
.Ft struct ctrlmenu
structure holding an array of menu entries that make the menu,
and the number of members in such array.
.Pp
Except for
.Fa entries
and
.Fa nentries ,
all the other members of
.Fa menu
must be zeroed before the first
(and only the first!)
call of
.Fn ctrlmenu_popup .
All the other members are filled by ctrlmenu routines.
.It Fa leader
The ID of the client window that the menu is a context of.
If the menu is not a context menu (or transient for a main window),
this argument should be
.Dv None .
.Pp
If an existing window is given,
it should be a descendant of the same root window of the
.Fa reference
one.
.It Fa reference
A window or pixmap whose screen, visual type, color depth, and colormap
are used for the menu window.
The window/pixmap itself is ignored;
it is only relevant for its attributes.
.Pp
You should just pass the root window,
unless you want to use a custom visual/depth/colormap for the menu,
in which case you must create a window with the needed attributes.
.Pp
.Em Rationale :
The reference window replaces four parameters that were used previously:
the screen number, the visual type, the color depth, and the colormap.
Most often, the programmer just wants to use the default values.
So, to simplify the API and make falling back to the defaults easier,
just pass a dummy reference window.
Lots of Xlib routines do that anyway
(but in their case that's mostly a need of the X protocol,
rather than the API design).
.It Fa resources
The resource database with configuration for the menu look and feel.
If its value is
.Dv NULL ,
resource is not processed.
.It Fa timestamp
The time of the event that triggered the menu to pop up.
.It Fa x_root , y_root
The
.Pq Em x,y
position where the menu is popped up.
.El
.Ss Menu structure
The
.Ft ctrlmenu
structure holds the definition of a menu.
It members are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width WINDOW
.It Fa entries , nentries
The array of entries of the submenu that is created when the entry is activated;
and the number of members of such array.
.It Fa title
The title of the menu
(to be displayed by the window manager if the menu gets windowed/torn-off).
.Pp
Usually,
for submenus this should be the label of the the menu entry that triggered it.
For example, if the entry
.Em Recent files
in the menu
.Em Files
opens a submenu, this submenu should probably be called
.Em Recent files .
It is up to the programmer to follow this good practice.
.It Fa window
The window that was created for the menu by
.Fn ctrlmenu_popup
or
.Fn ctrlmenu_tearoff .
Whenever the caller program gets an event for a window it does not directly manage,
it must loop through all its
.Ft "struct ctrlmenu"
objects, check if the
.Fa ".window"
member matches the event's window, and call
.Fn ctrlmenu_handle_event
on it.
.El
.Ss Entry structure
The
.Ft ctrlmenu_entry
structure holds the definition of a menu entry.
Its members are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fa callback , self
Function to be called when the entry is activated.
The opaque pointer
.Fa self
is given to the
.Fa callback
function.
These members must only be set on
.Dv CTRLMENU_COMMAND
entries.
.It Fa disabled
Whether the menu entry is disabled.
It is only relevant for command and cascade menu entries.
.It Fa label
Pointer to NUL-terminated string of UTF-8 characters to be displayed on the entry.
.It Fa submenu
The pointer to the menu that pops up when the entry is activated.
This member must only be set on
.Dv CTRLMENU_CASCADE
entries.
.It Fa type
The entry's type, represented by an enumeration constant:
.Bl -tag -width CTRLMENU_SEPARATOR
.It Dv CTRLMENU_COMMAND
When activated, the entry triggers calling the callback routine.
The member
.Fa callback
must be set on this type of entry.
.It Dv CTRLMENU_SEPARATOR
Entry is just a line separating entries above from entries below.
.It Dv CTRLMENU_CASCADE
When activated, the entry pops up a submenu.
The member
.Fa submenu
must be set on this type of entry.
.El
.Pp
See
.Sx USAGE
below for a complete list of entry types and their behavior,
including those not specified by the caller program,
and the disabled versions of command and submenu entries.
.El
.Pp
The members
.Fa callback Ns / Ns Fa self
and the member
.Fa submenu
must not be set at the same time,
for they are relevant for different types of entries each
.Po
.Dv CTRLMENU_COMMAND
and
.Dv CTRLMENU_CASCADE ,
respectively
.Pc .
In fact, they are in anonymous structs in an anonymous union,
if the compiler supports it:
.Bd -literal
union {
	struct {
		void (*callback)(void *ctx, void *self, bool alt);
		void *self;
	};
	struct {
		struct ctrlmenu *submenu;
	};
};
.Ed
.Ss Callback function
The
.Fa callback
member of a
.Vt struct ctrlmenu_entry
is the pointer to a function which is called when the entry is activated,
and its type is
.Dv CTRLMENU_COMMAND .
What that function actually does is up to the program.
.Pp
The
.Fa callback
function has the following type:
.Bd -filled -offset indent
.Ft void
.Fo callback
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fa "void *self"
.Fa "bool alt"
.Fc
.Ed
.Pp
When called,
the opaque pointers
.Fa ctx
and
.Fa self
are given to the
.Fa callback
function.
The idea is that
.Fa ctx
is a context object holding the current status of the program, while
.Fa self
is some data relevant for that particular entry.
But the program can use them the way it likes.
.Pp
If the entry is activated with the left mouse button,
or with the Enter or right-arrow key,
the value of the
.Fa alt
argument is
.Dv false .
If the entry is activated with the middle mouse button,
or with the Enter or right-arrow key while Shift is pressed,
the value of the
.Fa alt
argument is
.Dv true.
The idea is that the program can do a different action on an
.Dq alternate activation .
But it is not required to do so.
.Ss Popup function
The
.Fa ctrlmenu_popup
function pops up a menu with the given
.Fa entries ,
and blocks until the menu is closed
(either by activating an entry or closing the menu).
.Sh USAGE
A menu is a window listing entries for the user to select.
The types of menu entries are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
.It Em Command entry
A labelled button which, when activated, invokes the callback routine.
.It Em Cascade entry
A labelled entry with a rightwards-pointing triangle after which,
when activated, pops up a submenu.
.It Em Disabled command entry
Same as a command entry,
but it is displayed dimmed and cannot be activated.
.It Em Disabled cascade entry
Same as a cascade entry,
but it is displayed dimmed and cannot be activated.
.It Em Separator entry
A dimmed line that separates the entries above it from the entries below it.
Separator entries cannot be activated, and have no use other than display.
.It Em Tear-off entry
A single dashed line at the top of a pop-up menu which,
when activated, it tears the menu off.
Unlike other menu entries, it is not specified by the programmer,
but created automatically by the ctrlmenu routines.
The tear-off entry only appears on pop-up menus, if enabled by resources.
.It Em Scroller entries
A pair of upwards and downwards pointing triangles
(one at the top, the other at the bottom) which,
when activated or hovered, scroll the entries upwards or downwards.
Unlike other menu entries, they are not specified by the programmer,
but created automatically by the ctrlmenu routines.
The scroller entries only appear
if there are more entries on the menu than the monitor can display.
.El
.Pp
A menu can be either a pop-up menu or a torn-off menu.
.Bl -tag -width xxxxxxxxxxxxx
.It Em pop-up menu
Menu is not managed by the window manager
(i'e', its window is override-redirect).
The keyboard and pointer are grabbed by a pop-up menu while it is open.
When an entry is selected, the pop-up menu is closed.
.It Em torn-off menu
Menu is managed by the window manager, like a regular window.
The input is not grabbed.
Any number of entries can be selected, as long as the menu window is not closed.
.El
.Pp
Menus are controlled as follows:
.Bl -column -compact xxxxxxxxxxxxxxx XXXXXXXXXXXXXX xxx
.It Sy Target           Ta Sy Operation         Ta Sy Description
.It Tear-off entry      Ta Activate             Ta Tears off pop-up menu.
.It Cascade entry       Ta Activate             Ta Pops up submenu.
.It Command entry       Ta Activate             Ta Calls the entry's callback routine.
.It Command entry       Ta Alt-activate         Ta Calls the entry's callback routine,
for alternate action.
.It Scroll-up entry     Ta Hover pointer        Ta Scrolls entries upwards.
.It Scroll-down entry   Ta Hover pointer        Ta Scrolls entries downwards.
.It Any entry           Ta Hover pointer        Ta Selects entry (if possible).
.It Close button*       Ta Left click*          Ta Closes torn-off menu.
.It Outside menu        Ta Left/right click     Ta Closes pop-up menu.
.It Selected entry      Ta Left/right click     Ta Activates the selected entry.
.It Selected entry      Ta Middle click         Ta Activates the selected entry for alternate action.
.It Selected entry      Ta Hold Li Shift        Ta "If entry is"
activated while
.Ql Shift
is pressed,
activate for alternate action.
.It Selected entry      Ta Li Right Ns / Ns Li Enter No key      Ta Activates the selected entry.
.It Anywhere            Ta Li Home No key       Ta Selects the first entry.
.It Anywhere            Ta Li End  No key       Ta Selects the last entry.
.It Anywhere            Ta Li Down Ns / Ns Li Tab No key  Ta Selects the next entry,
cycling back to the first one.
.It Anywhere            Ta Li "Up\&" Ns / Ns Li Shift-Tab No key  Ta Selects the previous entry,
cycling back to the last one.
.It Anywhere            Ta Li Left Ns / Ns Li Esc No key         Ta Closes topmost pop-up menu.
.El
.Pp
*depends on the window manager:
Some use double-click on window's menu to close it;
others do not even render a close button on windows' titlebar
(or a titlebar at all).
.Sh RESOURCES
Refer to the
.Xr X 7
manual, at the section
.Sx RESOURCES
for more information on X resources.
.Bl -column XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX XXX
.It Sy Resource name                Ta Sy Default value  Ta Sy Type
.It Dv activeBackground  Ta Dv #3465A4   Ta color
.It Dv activeForeground  Ta Dv #FFFFFF   Ta color
.It Dv alignment         Ta Dv left      Ta Brq Qo Dv left Qc , Qo Dv center Qc , Qo Dv right Qc
.It Dv background        Ta Dv #313131   Ta color
.It Dv borderWidth       Ta Dv 1         Ta size (in pixels)
.It Dv font Ns / Ns Dv faceName     Ta (system's default)  Ta xft-font Sy OR No xldf-font
.It Dv fontSize Ns / Ns Dv faceSize Ta (system's default)  Ta size (in points)
.It Dv foreground        Ta Dv #FFFFFF   Ta color
.It Dv maxItems          Ta (computed)   Ta positive integer
.It Dv opacity           Ta 1.0          Ta Bq 0.0 , 1.0
.It Dv shadowThickness   Ta Dv 1         Ta size (in pixels)
.It Dv tearOff           Ta Dv false     Ta boolean
.It Dv topShadowColor    Ta (computed)   Ta color
.It Dv bottomShadowColor Ta (computed)   Ta color
.El
.Pp
For the syntax of color resources,
refer to the
.Xr X 7
manual, at the section
.Sx COLOR NAMES .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic alignment
If set to
.Qq Ic left ,
.Qq Ic center ,
or
.Qq Ic right ,
text is aligned to the left, center, or right of the menu, respectively.
By default, text is aligned to the left.
.It Ic background , activeBackground
The color of the background for the menu and its selected entry,
respectively.
.It Ic borderColor
The color of the border around the menu.
.It Ic borderWidth
The size in pixels of the border around the menu.
.It Ic font Pq synonym: Ic faceName
The font for drawing text.
.Pp
If it is prefixed with
.Qq Ic xft: ,
it is a
.Em FontConfig
font pattern;
and the Xft library is used to draw text.
Fallback fonts, for missing glyphs, can be specified by delimiting the fonts with commas.
Refer to the
.Xr fonts.conf 5
manual, at the section
.Sx FONT NAMES ,
for the syntax of font pattern strings.
.Pp
If it is prefixed with
.Qq Ic x:
or
.Qq Ic x11: ,
it is a
.Em X Logical Font Description
.Pq Em XLFD
font pattern;
and the X11 library is used to draw text.
Refer to the
.Xr fonts.conf 5
manual, at the section
.Sx FONT NAMES ,
for the syntax of font pattern strings.
.Pp
If no prefix is given,
.Nm
first tries the resource as a XLFD pattern, and then as a Xft pattern.
.It Ic fontSize Pq synonym: Ic faceSize
The size, in points of the font.
This only affects
.Xr Xft 3
fonts.
.It Ic foreground , activeForeground
The color of the label text on the menu and its selected entry,
respectively.
If not set, these resources are a contrasting color computed from
.Ic background
and
.Ic activeBackground ,
respectively.
.It Ic maxItems
Maximum number of entries to be displayed in a menu.
If a menu has more than this number of entries, they will be scrolled with arrow buttons.
The actual number of entries displayed may be lower to fit the screen.
.It Ic opacity
Background opacity as a floating point number between
0.0 (transparent) and 1.0 (opaque) inclusive.
.It Ic shadowThickness
The size in pixels of the Motif-like 3D relief.
.It Ic tearOff
If
.Qq Ic true ,
creates a dashed line on the top of each non-windowed menu to tear them off into a new windowed menu.
.It Ic topShadowColor , bottomShadowColor
The color of the top/light and bottom/dark parts of the Motif-like 3D relief.
If not set, these resources are computed from
.Ic background .
.El
